Date: Tue, 18 Jan 94 11:42:35 -0800
From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: BOT: How do trees drink

Robert Brooks talks about redwoods and fog:

> [...]

I think I recall from my botany class, that the redwood/fog interaction
consists in great part of fog condensing on the redwood foliage, dripping
off and watering the ground, thence via the roots to the rest of the tree.
Unnecessarily roundabout, except that one function of sap circulation in
a tree is to bring nutrients from the soil up to the rest of the plant.

I recall riding my bicycle along a road high in the San Francisco Peninsula
on a foggy day (highway 35, down toward 17, for those who know the Bay
Area.  Actually, it's still highway 35, down toward 17, even if you don't
know the Bay Area...).  When the road passed through stands of redwoods,
there was enough precipitation off the foliage to feel like light to
moderate rain.  But away from the big trees -- even at the same elevation
and the same density of fog (same visibility, or lack of it), it was just
damp.
                                          -- Jay Freeman

