Date: Tue, 18 Jan 1994 12:17:19 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Extropian love

Vince Kerchner makes some interesting comments towards a theory
of relationships, including:

> ...how can fundamentally non-rational
> constructs such as emotions, and the emotion of love (or, for some of
> you, the emotion of hate) in particular, be integrated/accomodated in
> such a system?"

This gets to the heart of the matter, so to speak.  Rational theories
of human relationships need to be falsified in terms of human emotions, 
aesthetics, instincts, etc. just as rational theories of other aspects of 
nature need to be falsified against the concrete reality observed in those
phenomenon.  

Theories of, for example, hormonal chemical networks in abstract
isolation can become too complex for any single human to understand.
The full range of human emotions and aethetics might be vastly more
complex still.  It's possible that good theories of relationships 
are so logically deep that:

* They cannot be expressed or understand by our human brains
without very lossy compression, which probably takes the form
of metaphorical reference to similarly complex phenomenon;
* It's computationally infeasible to create such a theory over
a single (unaugmented) human lifetime;
* Some theories can be learned only by trial and error, where the error
takes a form prohibitively severe for most individuals, such as
being stuck in an abusive relationship, raising a family and
having it go wrong, or even being assaulted or killed when a 
poorly constructed relationship goes sour. 

If so, the very compressed theories of previous generations provide
vastly more sophisticated and useful guide for relationships than
any we could generate ourselves, from scratch.  Theories compressed
into tradtion might take the form of conclusions stripped away from the 
complex series of observations, experiences, lessons learned, reasonings,
etc. which led up to them, and thus look irrational.  

Given such subtly valuable traditions, there is still a need improving 
these theories with our own experiences, and adapting them to modern
technology.   This might require "memetic archaeology", determining
why these traditions emerged in previous generations when there is
no obvious rational reason for them, and indeed the _prima facia_
explanation might be highly irrational.  On the other hand it
might be extremely entropic to use theories created from scratch 
with our current very limited brains instead of using the vast, 
logically deep storehouse of lessons that billions of humans have 
already learned.

Nick Szabo				szabo@netcom.com

