Vince "We want information" Kerchner <vincek@netcom.com> says-

> Specifically, we ought to be able to run AppleShare over internet, and
> thereby do all our networking operations as if they were being
> performed on the Mac desktop.

This part can be done with existing software in a couple of ways:
	Run AppleShare on top of NFS on top of TCP/IP to Netcom
	Run AppleShare --> Ethertalk --> TCP/IP -->Netcom,
           with an AppleShare server at Netcom
        Run AppleShare --> NFS or Ethernet on the internet at large??
(All of these would require SLIP or PTP, both of which can automatically
dial your modem and make you connected to internet when you need it.)
(I don't know the full details on this stuff.  At work we have Eudora
over our ethernet, Apple Unix File Server on a Sun, some Macs that
run Unix, and a Telnet/FTP program on the non-Unix Macs.)

Vince gets into more stuff, though...
	Having everything appear on a single volume on his Netcom
	   account, even though some of it lives further away
	Having the list software get involved.
These are trickier.  Real soon now Apple will have a link between the
Finder (the desktop icon and file manager) and AppleScript, their
scripting language.  When that's available you can pretty much make
anything appear anywhere and act like anything, I think.

I'm not totally in love with the Mac interface.  For instance, for a
while I was using Eudora to store notes to myself in preference to the
Finder.  I just found the scrolling windows with long subject lines,
the ease of creating a new message, the easy transition between that 
and the mail (ie no transition), and the way it remembers which 
things I had already dealt with, to be nicer than the Finder.  I kinda
wish somebody would come up with an interface that combined filing,
windows, mail, linking, remote access and object-oriented stuff (double 
click and something active or interactive starts to happen) and was 
workable on all the kinds of machines people have.

The current state of everything in computers makes me grouchy.
But on the other hand sometimes its surprising what's out there and
how well some things work out.

> We'd
> need to write a Finder extension which would manage the Chooser
> selection of and connection to the local internet server.  That should
> be pretty straight-forward.  Next, we'd need to write a driver which
> would access the modem and mount it as a normal AppleShare disk.

Like I said, both of these are done.  Actually it's a little different.
The SLIP or PTP software contains an entry for each computer (eg
Netcom) you might want to talk to.  When you pick that computer in the
Chooser, that's when I think it dials the modem, and when the 
connection's made, you see the list of volumes, assuming there's 
something on the other end offering them.

> The Unix side would be a little harder.  We'd need to write a new
> program which could be launched over the normal tty device driver to
> interface with our Mac driver.  It would have to translate between
> Unix directory format (by reading I-nodes) and directories and files
> on the Mac.

This is the combination of the other ends of either SLIP or PTP,
and either {EtherTalk and a Unix/Mac fileserver} or NFS.  All exist.

> In essence, it would manage the datastream necessary to
> represent the Unix directory tree and permission system as a graphical
> user interface on the Mac, and vice-versa.  It would also have to
> manage the interface and translation between the multiple creator and
> file types, icon and other Finder info, and resource fork, of Mac
> files, and the text or binary file representation of the server.  In
> addition, both the Mac and Unix drivers may have to transparently
> manage the binhexing/unbinhexing of binary data from the Mac to the
> server when the tty driver is in ASCII mode.

There are two standard translations between Unix and Mac directory and
file formats, called "Apple Single" (everything stuffed in one file) and 
"Apple Double" (some things in files in hidden parallel subdirectories).  
The NFS mounter would use one of those on the Mac side, or an AFP server 
would do one of those on the Unix side.

It's all completely transparent, totally integrated, real soon now.  
Bugs?  What bugs?

