Date: Tue, 18 Jan 94 16:44:00 EST
From: Harvey Newstrom <hnewstrom@hnewstrom.ess.harris.com>
Subject: Net tech

Vince Kerchner writes:
>I'm not very sorry for presenting my networking scenario in such a
>vendor-specific way.  For me, seemless integration of networking and
>internet services into my OS and everything else I do on my computer
>is very important.  That is, I really do want my internet front end to
>be an extension of AppleShare.  I have no idea what those of you using
>inferior hardware and operating systems will use.

My point was that I use NFS on my Mac.  I mount a remote disk as an icon
on my desktop.  When I open it, I see folders and documents just like on
the Mac.  You can't tell whether I am using NFS or Appletalk to mount the 
disk.  PC-Windows users can mount the same disk simultaneously and see it
open up like a windows icon.  Unix users can mount the same disk and see
it when they do unix commands just like a unix disk.

The protocol behind your desktop should not matter.  I use NFS because it
runs much faster.  Being part of the IP protocol definition, it allows
all packets to be sent as fast as they can be sent.  Any lost packets can
be resent.  Appletalk has a poor design in that it sends a packet and must 
wait for an acknowledgement before it sends the next packet.  This inserts 
the total round-trip delay time for a packet between every packet!

>It sounds like the closest thing to what I'm looking for is NFS,
>extended with some GUI.  Again, I advocate seemless integration with
>the front end system, which means that I don't want to run X.

NFS/Share from Intercon(tm) does this already for Macs.  PC/TCP already
does this for PCs.  Unix has this built in.  All systems seemlessly 
integrate the disk sharing capability into their OS model.

>The underlying mechanism for access to the
>greatest number of systems will probably have to be ftp-based, so we
>need to extend my scenario to include transparent operation of such a
>low-level file transmission program as part of the "drag-and-drop"
>interface which manifests on the Mac.

I agree that FTP programs for the Mac and other platforms should make the 
access seemless, and the commands like those used in the native 
environment.  All the functionality is already there, we just need to
present it to the user so it looks like the rest of the system.
