What is normally done?

Provocative Therapy, by Frank Farrelly, describes good ways of
establishing reasonable boundaries in other people.  His approach is
to confront people with the consequences of inappropriately defined
boundaries in a visceral and humorous way.  Doing it viscerally
ensures that the person pays attention and learns something, and doing
it humorously keeps the relationship going.

Here's an example quoted from memory: A female patient enters Frank's
office and says "Where should I sit?".  Frank says "Well, sit over
there."  Just before she can sit down, Frank says "No, on second
thought, sit over there instead.".  Just before she sits down over
there instead, Frank apparently changes his mind again.  This goes on
until she makes up her own mind about where to sit.

Tim May's BlackNet hoax (It *was* a hoax, right?) had a similar tone.

Can't say whether it's extropic; I like it because unlike other ways
of manipulating people (Neuro Linguistic Programming, e.g.), it
doesn't involve doing anything sneaky, it can be done unilaterally if
it can be done at all, and it respects other people's
self-determination.

